        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Picts</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> and the Forth-Clyde isthmus, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> in the west; the core of Fortriu lay around Moray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>; sites include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghead">Burghead</a> Fort, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portmahomack">Portmahomack</a>, Abernethy, Rosemarkie and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">the name Picti from the late third century CE (first in a panegyric of 297); several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, then the Verturian hegemony of Fortriu from the seventh century, lasting about 160 years; merger with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> into the Kingdom of Alba in the ninth century; Pictish identity forgotten, probably by the 11th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Pictish symbol stones and cross-slabs; the Pictish Beast and the mirror and comb, double-disc and Z-rod and crescent and V-rod symbols; heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> chains; penannular brooches and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-ninians-isle">St Ninian's Isle</a> Treasure; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Battle of Dun Nechtain</a> in 685; an Insular Celtic language now extinct</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">outside writers: Roman panegyrics and Claudian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s Ecclesiastical History, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>'s life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>, the Irish annals and the Pictish Chronicle; the stones and their inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and ogham; excavations such as Martin Carver's at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portmahomack">Portmahomack</a>; a 2023 study of eight whole genomes from Lundin Links and Balintore</span></div>
