    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The name Picti appears in Roman sources from the end of the third century CE as an outsiders' term for unromanised people in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and was most likely pejorative.</li><li>From the seventh century the kingdom of Fortriu, centred on Moray, dominated Pictland, and in the ninth century the Pictish kingdom merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> to form the Kingdom of Alba.</li><li>The Pictish language, an Insular Celtic language related to Brittonic, was gradually displaced by Gaelic from the late ninth century, and Pictish identity was forgotten, probably during the 11th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word Picti is most commonly explained as meaning 'painted', yet the nobles, hunters and warriors carved on Pictish stones are shown without obvious tattoos.</p></aside>
    </section>

